A||||13||DavidHenshaw||What is correct idle rpm||||1||
Z||000000||DavidHenshaw||05-16-2001||09:50 PM||||What would be correct idle rpm for a V6 automatic in neutral with warm engine and also when in drive with warm engine.<P>I'm reading 1700 in neutral and about 1100 while in drive. I'm sure this is too high.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jay||05-16-2001||09:57 PM||||rpm at idle should be just under 1000rpm.  I'd say between 900-950.  Where are you getting the reading from the instrument cluster or off of the wire?<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande||||reg||1
Z||000002||PBJ||05-16-2001||10:20 PM||||I agree with Jay.  idle should read about 1000 or under on tach-if tach is accurate.  My wifes v6 idled too high because of a seized IAC and coolant temp sensor reading 15 deg cooler.  could be a vacume leak.  could be inaccurate gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||05-17-2001||12:50 AM||||The ECM target is 900 RPM.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LarryB||05-17-2001||12:57 AM||||Dunno where you guys are getting your figures but according to my '86 and '87 Helms, for the 2.8:<P>               '86                '87<P>neutral       900 +/- 50 rpm 900 +/- 75 rpm<BR>drive         800 +/- 50 rpm 800 +/- 75 rpm<P><BR>On the V6 if the warm idle is more than 75 rpm over the correct idle speed for more than 45 seconds (on the 4 cyl its 50 rpm for 30 secs) you're *supposed* to see a code 35.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||05-17-2001||06:05 PM||||LarryB that's about what we said 900 to 950, but unfortunately the gauges are not as exact as taking a reading off of the wire.  So the rpm could be correct thus not setting off a code but the gauge is showing a lower or higher reading.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande||||reg||1
